{"id":"smoke_trail","compound_name":"Graphs::Aero::smoke_trail","source_path":"smoke_trail/smoke_trail.json","category":"Smoke","author":"Autodesk","description":"An aero simulation producing a smoke trail","documentation_file":"smoke_trail_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Smoke Trail Graph\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph uses aero to create a smoke trail simulation\n\n### Inputs and outputs \n\nInputs for this graph are the objects which will emit the smoke trails.  The output is the resulting aero smoke trail volume.\n\n### Useful information\n\n- The smoke trail effect is accomplished by relying on the animated object's velocity to produce the look of thrust by the smoke.  By setting the `inherit velocity` to negative values, the smoke will be pushed in the opposite direction to the object's movement. Varying the force of this negative inherit will produce a lot of natural turbulence in the flow, which will cause the trail to spread out over time.\n\n- Because objects of all sizes and speeds may produce smoke trails, scene scale is crucial in obtaining the correct behavior, and maximizing the smoothness of the trail.  Set `scene units in meters` accordingly, or adjust your geometry.\n\n- Performance is related to the `fluid detail size` on the source air node, the magnitude and mode of the velocity, and how smooth the trail is. Different speed modes on the source will affect the negative inherit in dramatic ways, so watch out for that for that, as extreme velocities take longer to compute.  The speed on the object itself will affect the smoothness of the trail, which can be corrected by increasing the `trail smoothness` on the source and/or the `max steps`, both of which have performance impact.  It is highly recommended to use adaptive resolution regions for long, slowly dissipating smoke trails.  \n\n### Example scene\n\n- The example scene is a missile trail which uses a rocket geo animated along a curve.  Resolution boxes have been set up along the curve to reduce the detail size as the rocket gets further away from the camera.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","file":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"out_geometry","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"},{"portName":"mesh_out","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"counts":{"nodes":20,"connections":23,"values":76,"compounds":2},"node_types":{"Core::FCurve::evaluate_fcurve":3,"Simulation::Common::vary_source_property":2,"Simulation::Influence::modulate_influence":1,"Simulation::Influence::dissipation_influence":1,"Simulation::Influence::vorticity_influence":1,"Simulation::Influence::drag_influence":1,"Simulation::Aero::aero_solver_settings":1,"Simulation::Aero::source_air":1,"Simulation::Common::collider":1,"Simulation::Aero::simulate_aero":1,"Rendering::Materials::assign_material":1,"animated_sphere":1,"Simulation::Common::file_cache":1,"Rendering::Arnold::Arnold_standard_volume_mat":1,"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_sphere":1,"Core::Conversion::scalar_to_vector3":1,"Core::Time::time":1},"edge_type_pairs":{"Core::Time::time -> Core::FCurve::evaluate_fcurve":3,"Core::FCurve::evaluate_fcurve -> Core::Conversion::scalar_to_vector3":3,"Simulation::Aero::source_air -> Simulation::Aero::simulate_aero":1,"Simulation::Aero::aero_solver_settings -> Simulation::Aero::simulate_aero":1,"Simulation::Influence::modulate_influence -> Simulation::Influence::drag_influence":1,"Simulation::Influence::drag_influence -> Simulation::Influence::vorticity_influence":1,"Simulation::Common::collider -> Simulation::Aero::simulate_aero":1,"Simulation::Common::vary_source_property -> Simulation::Common::vary_source_property":1,"Simulation::Common::vary_source_prope